  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>TLV-TVM (Transports Littoral Varois) runs the shortest and most frequent service to Porquerolles, departing from La Tour Fondue on the Giens peninsula — a crossing of about 15 minutes. The company also serves Port-Cros and Le Levant, but from the port of Hyères (Port Gavine landing stage).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to TLV-TVM's FAQ (read 19/11/2026), animals travel free, dogs on a leash and other pets in a carrier; category 1 and 2 dogs must be muzzled and held on a leash by an adult. The timetable pages specify "dogs free but not allowed on the beaches". Once there, restrictions are set by the Port-Cros and Porquerolles National Park. On <strong>Porquerolles</strong>, the Park says dogs must be on a leash on paths and trails and are banned from the beaches. On <strong>Port-Cros</strong>, the Park says dogs are banned outside the village, where leashed access is allowed between the Fort du Moulin and the statue of Saint Joseph.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: Port-Cros is very restrictive for dogs (village only); check the National Park's regulations before choosing your island.</div>

      <h2>Tickets, crossings and season</h2>
      <p>According to TLV-TVM's FAQ, the crossing between La Tour Fondue and Porquerolles takes 15 minutes, and 45 minutes between Hyères and Port-Cros; strollers are free on board. The FAQ strongly advises booking in July and August, and boarding starts 15 minutes before departure. The 2026 timetable guide states that the vessels are wheelchair accessible, that carrying bikes is charged and that timetables change by period (31 August, 20 September, 2 November, 14 February). According to the Toulon Provence Méditerranée metropolitan authority, bus 67 of the Mistral network serves La Tour Fondue and is free in July-August for passengers who have booked their crossing.</p>
      <p><strong>Worth checking before you go:</strong> TLV-TVM fares and timetables vary by season and are available on tlv-tvm.com or at 04 94 58 21 81. For the restrictions on the islands, the reference is the Port-Cros and Porquerolles National Park, whose rule (dogs limited to the village on Port-Cros, leash on the paths of Porquerolles, banned on the beaches) is repeated above.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>According to the Toulon Provence Méditerranée metropolitan authority, La Tour Fondue is the main boarding point for Porquerolles, with departures all year round; other companies serve the island from La Londe-les-Maures, Toulon or Le Lavandou, each with its own conditions for dogs.</p>
